Rebrand the strings where the editor names itself Microsoft's product.

Product naming is not confined to product.json. Decoding the packaged
out/nls.messages.js against out/nls.metadata.json finds 150 strings carrying
"VS Code" or "Visual Studio Code", and a handful of them are the running
application referring to ITSELF by Microsoft's name on a surface a user of this
app reaches. Those are what this changes:

  * the onboarding overlay's hero title and its dialog aria-label,
    "Welcome to VS Code" and "Welcome to Visual Studio Code", which is the first
    screen of the 2026 onboarding
  * the three reload prompts after installing or uninstalling an extension,
    "Please reload Visual Studio Code to enable it", and its plural and
    uninstall variants
  * the profile-switch confirmation, "Switching a profile requires reloading
    VS Code"
  * the featured web walkthrough, "Get Started with VS Code for the Web" over a
    page titled "Setup VS Code Web", and the accessibility walkthrough beside it

The other 140 are deliberately left alone, and the distinction is not squeamish:
they are nominative references to Microsoft's product and its extension API,
which is what they describe. "For VS Code extensions, specifies the VS Code
version that the extension is compatible with" would become false if it named
this app; "The categories used by the VS Code gallery" describes a gallery this
app does not even use. Renaming those would trade a trademark question for an
accuracy one, and add 140 lines that conflict on every version bump.

Two more are left alone for a different reason. "Get started with VS Code" and
"Setup VS Code" belong to the walkthrough gated `when: '!isWeb'`, which never
renders in this build.

verify-server-tree.py asserts the absence of each rebranded string in the
packaged nls bundle, on both the build side and the fetch side, so an upstream
revision that reintroduces one fails rather than shipping.

The walkthrough's telemetry footer needs no equivalent edit: upstream gates it on
productService.enableTelemetry, which the branding overlay sets to false, so it
never renders here.

